<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#yavana">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yavana</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Yavanas</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Yavana is a term used in ancient Indian languages for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> and, later, for other westerners. Derived through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-persian">Old Persian</a> Yauna from 'Ionian', it appears in the edicts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashoka">Ashoka</a>, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> literature and in inscriptions recording gifts by Yavana donors to Buddhist caves of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a>. In medieval usage it came to denote foreigners generally, including Muslims.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yavana.jpg" alt="Yavana" width="202" height="193" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:MenanderCoinFront.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
